026. From January 2027 the matter moves to report drafting with four associates. Big investigations are hard on juniors; that is the nature of the work. Dorian brought Kestrel in and is exactly the partner this group needed. I would resist any account that makes this about him.\n- Regulatory Investigations associate pulse survey, July 2026 (15 of 20 responded) (anonymous-staff-survey): Scores out of 5, with the firm-wide average in brackets: 'My workload is sustainable' 2.3 (3.4). 'The partners I work for treat me with respect' 2.8 (3.9). 'I get useful feedback on my work' 2.9 (3.6). 'I am fairly paid relative to the market' 3.0 (new item; no firm comparison). Selected free-text comments: 'Months of weekends on the investigation. Nobody can keep this up.' — 'Drafts come back after midnight with corrections copied to the whole team. It's humiliating.' — 'Rival firms pay more for less.' — 'I'd stay if I could get off the big matter.' The survey tool does not record which matters respondents work on.\n- Management committee minutes, 6 October 2026 (extract) (document): Item 4, Regulatory Investigations attrition. The committee agreed to postpone a decision until the analysis is done. Options tabled: (a) a £12k retention payment for every associate in the group (GT), estimated cost £228k across 19 associates; (b) contract reviewers to take over Kestrel first-level review, about £160k absorbed by the firm because the client will not fund it (PL); (c) moving supervision of Kestrel associates to another partner (associate representative). HR reported that an external interviewer could hold confidential interviews with departed and current associates within three weeks for about £9k. Four of the nine leavers have already agreed to take part. Action: analyst to recommend.\n\nBRIEF:\nThe departures are concentrated in one place: 8 of the 11 associates on the Kestrel matter resigned, against 1 of 13 who never worked on it. Other groups look normal, and the billable-target rise